course: books
lesson: 64
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter X, part 4: The Further Adventures of Toad (the horse-pond, the chase and a face in the bank)
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Sitting by the driver, Toad asks sweetly if «she» may try driving, just to tell her friends. The driver laughs, but the gentleman says «Bravo, ma'am! I like your spirit.» Toad starts slowly and carefully, and the gentlemen clap: fancy a washerwoman driving so well!
explain: Then faster, and faster. When they call «Be careful, washerwoman!» Toad loses his head. He pins the driver down with his elbow, goes full speed and shouts who he really is: «I am the Toad, the motor-car snatcher, the prison-breaker, the Toad who always escapes!» The whole party jumps up to seize him — and nobody stops the car. With half a turn of the wheel it crashes through a hedge into a muddy horse-pond.
explain: Toad flies through the air like a swallow and lands on his back in soft grass; the gentlemen and the driver flounder in the pond in their long coats. Toad runs off, then laughs till he has to sit down, and sings again: «Who was it steered it into a pond? Ingenious Mr. Toad!» — «O, how clever I am! How clever, how clever, how very clev——»
explain: Two fields away, a chauffeur and two large country policemen are running after him! Toad runs, calling himself a conceited ass, looks back over his shoulder — and runs straight into the river. The current sweeps him along until he grabs the edge of a big dark hole in the bank. Something small and bright twinkles inside it, and a face grows round it: brown and small, with whiskers. It is the Water Rat!
fact: Toad asks: «I wish you would kindly let me try and drive the car for a little... I should like to be able to tell my friends that once I had driven a motor-car!»
fact: The gentleman: «Bravo, ma'am! I like your spirit. Let her have a try, and look after her. She won't do any harm.»
fact: At first Toad drives «very slowly and carefully», «for he was determined to be prudent» (prudent = careful and sensible).
fact: «Be careful, washerwoman!» annoys him and he begins «to lose his head» (to lose your head = to stop thinking sensibly).
fact: He pins the driver down with one elbow and puts on full speed; «the rush of air in his face... intoxicated his weak brain» (intoxicated = made wild, as if drunk).
fact: «Washerwoman, indeed! ... I am the Toad, the motor-car snatcher, the prison-breaker, the Toad who always escapes!»
fact: The party cry «Seize the Toad, the wicked animal who stole our motor-car!»; the book says they «should have remembered to stop the motor-car somehow before playing any pranks of that sort».
fact: With a half-turn of the wheel the car crashes through a low hedge into a horse-pond (a pond where horses drink) and is nearly sunk; the men, in their long coats, flounder (splash about clumsily) in the water.
fact: Toad flies «with the strong upward rush and delicate curve of a swallow» and wonders if he will turn into «a Toad-bird», then lands on his back in soft meadow grass.
fact: His new song: «The motor-car went Poop-poop-poop, / As it raced along the road. / Who was it steered it into a pond? / Ingenious Mr. Toad!» (ingenious = very clever at inventing things)
fact: He is cut off in the middle of «how very clev——» when he sees a chauffeur (a paid car driver) in leather gaiters and two large rural (country) policemen running after him, about two fields away.
fact: «What an ass I am! What a conceited and heedless ass! Swaggering again! Shouting and singing songs again!» (heedless = not paying attention to danger)
fact: Looking back over his shoulder, he runs off the edge of the bank and «found himself head over ears in deep water»: the river.
fact: In the water he promises: «if ever I steal a motor-car again! If ever I sing another conceited song»— and goes under.
fact: The ending repeats the Mole's first meeting with the Rat in chapter I: something twinkles in a hole, a face grows round it, «Brown and small, with whiskers» — «It was the Water Rat!»
fact: Talk in this lesson: «gassing» (old British slang, used by Toad himself) = chattering, boasting; «delulu» = living in a dream; «I'm dead» = that's so funny; «fixin' to» (US South) = about to; «gadgie» (North East England) = a man, a bloke.
exercise: What reason did Toad give for wanting to drive? | To tell his friends he had once driven a motor-car | To get home quicker, because his married daughter was waiting | To test the brakes, because he was a mechanic | The driver looked tired
exercise: How did the driver react to the idea at first? | He laughed heartily | He said yes at once | He got very angry and stopped the car in the road | He fainted
exercise: «Bravo, ma'am! I like your spirit.» Who said this? | One of the gentlemen | The chauffeur at the wheel | A policeman who was standing in the road by the hedge | The old barge-woman again
exercise: How did Toad drive at first? | Very slowly and carefully | At full speed from the start, in a cloud of dust | Backwards, by mistake, into a hedge | In wobbly circles round the road
exercise: «He was determined to be prudent.» What does «prudent» mean? | Careful and sensible | Proud and very boastful | Fast and rather reckless | Polite and well-dressed, as a lady should be
exercise: What did the gentlemen say when Toad drove well at first? | «Fancy a washerwoman driving a car as well as that, the first time!» | «Stop at once, before you kill us all!» | «Go faster!» | «Who are you?»
exercise: What warning made Toad «lose his head»? | «Be careful, washerwoman!» | «Mind the hedge!» | «Stop, thief!» | «There's a policeman behind us, slow down now, please!»
exercise: How did Toad keep the driver from stopping him? | He pinned him down with one elbow | He threw him out of the car | He tied him up with his shawl, very tightly | He gave him money
exercise: «I am the Toad, the motor-car snatcher, the ...» what? | Prison-breaker | Honest washerwoman | Clever horse-dealer | Best driver in all England, and on the river too
exercise: What did the party shout when they heard who he was? | «Seize him!... the wicked animal who stole our motor-car!» | «Bravo!» | «Faster!» | «Mind the pond, washerwoman, and the hedge beside it, and the cows!»
exercise: What mistake does the book say the party made? | They should have stopped the car before grabbing Toad | They should never have given a lift to a stranger in a bonnet on a lonely road | They should have hit him | They should have driven to the police
exercise: Where did the car end up? | In a horse-pond, nearly sunk | Upside down in a ditch | In the river by Rat's house | Against the gate of Toad Hall, with its lamps smashed
exercise: «The strong upward rush and delicate curve of a ...» what bird? | Swallow | A wild duck | A barn owl | Kingfisher, flashing blue over the river
exercise: What did Toad wonder as he flew through the air? | Whether he would turn into a Toad-bird | Whether he would land in the river | Whether he would be hurt | Whether the policemen could see him from the road
exercise: Where did Toad land? | On his back in soft meadow grass | In the pond, right next to the gentlemen and the driver and the car | On the roof of a cottage | In a tree
exercise: What were the gentlemen and the driver doing? | Floundering in the pond in their long coats | Chasing him at once across the field | Laughing | Calling the police on a telephone they kept in the back of the car
exercise: «Who was it steered it into a pond?» What is the answer in Toad's song? | Ingenious Mr. Toad | The driver of the car | The gentlemen in their long coats, who grabbed the wheel | Nobody, it was an accident
exercise: Where was Toad's song cut off? | In the middle of «how very clev——» | At the end of the third verse | Before it began | When he fell into the river, still singing loudly
exercise: Who was running after him? | A chauffeur and two large rural policemen | The barge-woman and her husband, with their dog running in front | The gipsy and his dog | The Chief Weasel's stoats
exercise: «What a conceited and heedless ass!» What does «heedless» mean? | Not paying attention to danger | Without a head | Brave | Very fast at running across fields
exercise: Why did Toad run into the river? | He was looking back over his shoulder and not where he was going | He wanted to swim home, because the river leads to Toad Hall | He was pushed | He thought it was a field
exercise: What did Toad grab to stop being swept away? | The edge of a big dark hole in the bank | A floating log | The reeds and rushes along the water's edge, which held firm | A boat
exercise: What did Toad see shining in the hole? | Some bright small thing, then a face round it | A lantern | A policeman's silver badge glinting in the dark | The moon on the water
exercise: Whose face was it? | The Water Rat's | Badger's front door | Mole's, at Mole End | Otter's, with a fish in his mouth
test: Jake, delulu (living in a dream): «Toad drove carefully the whole way, the gentlemen just panicked for no reason.» Put it right. | He went faster and faster, pinned the driver down and shouted who he was | Right, he was a perfect driver and never went faster than a horse trots | He stopped at every gate | He let the driver take over again
test: Why did «Be careful, washerwoman!» make Toad so cross? | He hated being thought a washerwoman and wanted to be the famous Toad | He was a very careful driver and thought he was going slowly enough | He did not hear it | The car was going slowly
test: What would have happened if Toad had kept quiet about who he was? | The party would not have jumped up, so the car might not have crashed | He would have been arrested at once by the gentlemen and taken to the police | The car would have flown | He would have been given the car
test: Mia, «I'm dead» (that's so funny): «the posh blokes flying into the pond!» Who actually flew through the air? | Toad flew; the men ended up in the pond with the car | Everybody flew, and they all landed in the soft grass of the meadow | Only the driver flew | Nobody flew, they all walked
test: Is it TRUE or FALSE? «After the crash, Toad was sorry and went to help the men in the pond.» | False: he ran off and then laughed at them, singing | True: he pulled them all out, one by one, and said he was sorry | True: he waited for the police | False: he stayed in the pond
test: «Ingenious Mr. Toad!» Is steering a car into a pond really ingenious? | No — it is just reckless; Toad turns his bad luck into boasting | Yes, it was a very clever plan he had worked out from the very start | Yes, because the pond was soft | No, because he could not swim
test: Why does the song stop at «how very clev——»? | He suddenly sees the chauffeur and policemen running towards him | He forgot the words of the last verse and had to stop | He started to cry | Rat told him to be quiet
test: Danny, fixin' to (about to) laugh: «Toad finally learnt his lesson in that river, right?» What is the best answer? | He promises «if ever I steal a motor-car again!» — but he has promised before | Yes, and he never, ever boasts again in the whole book, not even once | No, he never promises anything | He does not fall in a river
test: Looking back: in chapter I, how did the Mole first see the Rat? | A twinkle in a hole in the bank, then a face growing round it | The Rat rowed past in his blue and white boat, singing a song | The Rat knocked on his door | The Rat fell on him from a tree
test: Looking back: in chapter I, «Brown little face, with whiskers... It was the Water Rat!» What is the same now? | The same words describe Rat's face appearing in his hole — now for Toad | The Rat is now old and grey and wears spectacles like Badger | Toad sees the Mole | The face is Badger's this time
test: Looking back: in chapter VIII, an engine full of warders and policemen chased Toad. How did he escape that time? | He jumped from the train after a tunnel and hid in a wood | He swam a wide river with the police behind him | He hid in the coal | He drove a car away
test: Looking back: in chapter VI, Badger made Toad promise to give up motor-cars. What did Toad say afterwards? | That the very first motor-car he saw, «poop-poop! off I go in it!» | That he was truly sorry and would never touch a car again | That he would buy a boat | That he would never drive again
test: Looking back: in chapter II, after the first motor-car, the Rat said Toad was «possessed» by a new craze. Is Toad still «possessed» here? | Yes — even in hiding he cannot resist asking to drive | No, he is completely cured, and never thinks of cars again | No, he now hates cars | Yes, but only by boats
test: Which shows Toad's mood swings best in this part? | Proud song, then terror, then «what an ass I am!», then splash | Calm all the way, from the first mile to the last | Sad, then sleepy | Angry, then kind to the gentlemen
test: ★★★ The policemen were «about two fields off». If each field were 200 steps across, about how many steps behind Toad were they? | 400 | 200 | 2 | 100
test: ★★★ Count the names Toad calls himself in «I am the Toad, the motor-car snatcher, the prison-breaker, the Toad who always escapes!» after «the Toad». How many are there? | 3 | 2 | 4 | 1
test: ★★★ In this chapter Toad gets away from three lots of people. In what order? | The barge-woman, the car party, the chauffeur and policemen | The policemen, the barge-woman, the gipsy who sold the stew | The gipsy, the barge-woman, the Rat | The gaoler, the engine-driver, the barge-woman
exam: Identify the remark which, by the narrator's account, caused Toad to «lose his head». | «Be careful, washerwoman!» | «Bravo, ma'am!» | «Seize the Toad!» | «Fancy a washerwoman driving a car as well as that, the first time!»
exam: Ascertain the error which the narrator attributes to the occupants of the motor-car. | That they omitted to stop the vehicle before attempting to restrain Toad | That they permitted a stranger to sit at the front | That they failed to recognise Toad beneath his washerwoman's bonnet and gown | That they drove at excessive speed through the countryside
exam: State the final resting-place of the motor-car. | A horse-pond | A deep canal lock | The river by the Rat's dwelling | The inn-yard of the Red Lion
exam: Select the response which is consistent with the established facts concerning Toad's deliverance from the river. | He seized the edge of a hole in the bank, wherein the Water Rat appeared | He was hauled out by the policemen, who arrived upon the bank in pursuit | He swam unaided to Toad Hall | He was carried by the current to the weir
